# Wireworm — Game state

{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
The build owns the shape of the game's state entirely.

The one contract fixed over the state is the debugging and automation surface
specified for this build. Every operation that surface carries is a read or a
pose of the running game, so the state holds what those operations read, honors
what they write, and returns to its title-screen values when the surface's
`reset` is called. Beyond satisfying that surface, how the state is organized is
yours to decide.

Whatever its shape, the state carries the following.

## The screen and the run

- The screen the game is showing, one of `title`, `howto`, `playing`, `paused`,
  `victory`, and `gameover`.
- The sub-phase of the `playing` screen, one of `banner`, `active`, and
  `respawn`, and the seconds left in that phase.
- The item highlighted on whichever vertical menu the current screen shows,
  counted from `0`.
- The score, the lives remaining, the level being played, and the highest level
  the run has reached.

## The board

- Every node standing on the board, each with the tile it occupies and its
  charge.
- Every worm, each with a distinct id, its segments in order from the head, its
  horizontal and vertical headings, its diving flag, and the two faculty gates
  the debug surface poses. Each worm also carries its own step clock, which is
  the accumulator `specs/worm.md` describes.
- Every foe, each with a distinct id, its kind, its center, its current velocity,
  the dropper's taken-its-first-bolt flag, and the two faculty gates the debug
  surface poses. A glitch also carries its own dart clock.
- Every bolt in flight, each with a distinct id and its center.
- The arcs of a live discharge, each naming the two tiles it joins and how much
  of its life is left.
- The cursor: its center, the seconds of spawn-in invulnerability left, and
  whether its contact test is running.
- The seconds until the cursor may fire again.

## The level's own spawners

- Whether the level's foe spawning is running, and whether the level's and the
  respawn's worm entry is running. Both are on when a run begins.
- The clocks the level's own spawners keep: the glitch's arrival interval, the
  corruptor's arrival interval, and the dropper's sparse-field check.

## The rest

- The simulation time, in seconds, which every update adds its delta to whatever
  the screen.
- Whether sound is muted, refreshed from the runtime's mute bit in every update.
- The state of the seeded random generator, which the starting scatter, the
  foes' arrival intervals, and the arcs' drawn shapes are all drawn from.
- Whatever the game needs to hand a fresh id to the next worm, foe, or bolt it
  creates, so no two entities live at one moment share an id.

The level's step interval and the level's worm length are derived from the level
by the formulas in `specs/worm.md` rather than stored.
