{{else}}
`src/game.ts` declares and exports the state the game is written against. This
section is that declaration: write it into `src/game.ts` exactly as it stands
here. The state is the world's game state: a class extending the engine's
`GameState`, named by the game mode as its `gameStateClass`, so the engine builds
one instance of it when the world opens and `engine.world.state` is that
instance. Wherever this specification writes `state.foo`, it means a field of
that instance.

The game runs in one world for the whole session. The game definition registers a
single level, the game never opens another, and every screen is a value of
`screen`, so the state below is built once and lives until the page closes. The
framework's states are live objects: a tick writes the fields it advances in
place, and each field initializer below is that field's title-screen value, the
same value the debug surface's `reset` restores. `GameState` comes from the
engine's package, and `START_LIVES` and `DEFAULT_SEED` from `src/constants.ts`.

