## The screen and the run

- `screen` — the screen currently shown, one of the six `specs/ui.md` defines.
- `phase` — the sub-phase of the `playing` screen, as `specs/progression.md`
  defines it. It holds `banner` on every other screen.
- `phaseTimer` — the seconds left in the current phase, counting down against
  each update's delta and resting at `0`.
- `menuIndex` — the highlighted item on whichever vertical menu `screen` is
  showing, counted from `0`. It rests at `0` on `playing`.
- `score`, `lives`, `level` — the run's figures.
- `reachedLevel` — the highest level the run has opened, which is what the end
  screens report.

## `NodeState`

One node and the tile it occupies. Only a tile holding a node has an entry, so an
empty tile appears nowhere.

- `c`, `r` — the tile the node occupies.
- `charge` — a whole number from `0` to `CHARGE_MAX` (`3`).

`nodes` lists every node on the board in ascending row, then ascending column.

## `WormState`

One worm on the board.

- `id` — distinct among every entity live at that moment, and kept for as long as
  the worm exists.
- `segments` — the tiles the worm occupies, in order from the head, so
  `segments[0]` is the head and the last entry is the tail.
- `dh` — the horizontal heading, `+1` for right and `-1` for left.
- `dv` — the vertical heading, `+1` for down and `-1` for up.
- `diving` — whether the worm is driving straight down its column.
- `stepping` — whether the worm's step runs, which the debug surface poses. It is
  `true` for a worm the game itself brought in.
- `body` — whether the body's follow runs, which the debug surface poses. It is
  `true` for a worm the game itself brought in.
- `stepClock` — the worm's own step accumulator, in seconds, as `specs/worm.md`
  describes it.

`worms` lists every worm on the board, and a worm added through the debug surface
is appended to it.

## `FoeState`

One foe on the board.

- `id` — distinct among every entity live at that moment, and kept for as long as
  the foe exists.
- `kind` — `"glitch"`, `"dropper"`, or `"corruptor"`.
- `x`, `y` — the foe's center.
- `vx`, `vy` — the velocity the foe's position is changing by right now, in
  logical units per second, including any dart. A foe whose horizontal motion
  reverses reports a `vx` that reverses sign with it.
- `hit` — whether a dropper has taken its first bolt. It is `false` for the other
  two kinds.
- `mind` — whether the foe's own behavior runs, which the debug surface poses. It
  is `true` for a foe the game itself brought in.
- `travel` — whether the foe's locomotion runs, which the debug surface poses. It
  is `true` for a foe the game itself brought in.
- `dartClock` — a glitch's own dart accumulator, in seconds. It rests at `0` for
  the other two kinds.

## `BoltState`, `ArcState`, and `CursorState`

- A bolt carries an `id`, distinct among every entity live at that moment, and
  its center.
- An arc carries the two tiles it joins and `life`, the seconds of its
  `ARC_LIFE` (`0.32` s) that remain.
- The cursor carries its center, `invulnerable`, the seconds of spawn-in
  invulnerability left, and `contact`, whether its contact test runs, which the
  debug surface poses.

## The level's own spawners

- `foeSpawning` — whether the level's own spawning of foes runs, which the debug
  surface poses.
- `wormEntry` — whether the level's and the respawn's entry of a worm runs, which
  the debug surface poses.
- `glitchTimer`, `corruptorTimer`, `dropperTimer` — the clocks those spawners
  keep, as `specs/foes.md` describes them.

## The rest

- `nextId` — the id the next worm, foe, or bolt takes, so no two entities live at
  one moment share one.
- `simTime` — accumulated simulation time, in seconds. Every update adds its
  delta, whatever the screen.
- `muted` — the game's readable copy of the runtime's mute bit, refreshed in
  every update. The runtime owns muting, and this field is what the debug surface
  reports.
- `rngState` — the state of the game's seeded random generator. The starting
  scatter, the foes' arrival intervals and entry positions, and the drawn shape of
  an arc are all drawn from it.

The level's step interval and the level's worm length are derived from `level` by
the formulas in `specs/worm.md` rather than stored.

## The contract

The declaration above is what the debug surface reads and poses.

- Keep every field under its declared name, type, and meaning.
{{#if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}
- `initialize` builds the whole state in one go, so every field is present by the
  time a frame can observe it, and none of them is optional.
- The state handed to `update` is the whole of the game, and the state `update`
  returns is the whole of the next frame. Every value the game carries from one
  frame to the next lives in it; nothing is kept in a module-level variable or a
  closure.
- The state is handed over read-only, as `DeepReadonly<WirewormState>`, to
  `update`, to `render`, to every diagnostic source, and to every operation of the
  debug surface. `update` and the surface's poses return a new `WirewormState`
  built from the one they received; `render`, the sources, and `snapshot` return
  no state.
{{else}}
- The engine builds the state when the world opens, through the mode's
  `gameStateClass`, so every field is present by the time a frame can observe it,
  and none of them is optional.
- The state is the whole of the authoritative game. Every value the game carries
  from one frame to the next lives in it; nothing is kept in a module-level
  variable or a closure, and any field an actor, component, or controller holds of
  its own is derived data rebuilt from the declared ones.
- The state is written only through the paths play runs on: the player controller
  resolves the frame's input into it, the game mode's tick advances it, and the
  debug surface's poses arrange it at the call. Rendering reads it and writes
  nothing.
{{/if}}
- Fields you add hold derived data you can rebuild from the declared ones. A
  `reset` restores the declared fields to their title-screen values, so those
  fields are the whole of the authoritative state.
{{/if}}
